<SectionLayout
	title="About the Chromosomal Instability Network"
	pageTitle="About the Network"
	description="A community of thirty-seven laboratories advancing chromosomal instability research from the bench to the clinic."
>
	<section class="block">
		<h2>What is CIN?</h2>
		<p>
			Chromosomal instability (CIN) is one of the hallmarks of cancer. It broadly refers to any
			process that causes repeated generation of karyotype alterations from kilobase scale to whole
			chromosomes. It is an ongoing <strong>rate of change</strong>, which sets it apart from
			aneuploidy or karyotype complexity often seen in cacner genomes.
		</p>
		<p>
			CIN drives cancer formation and therapy resistance, but it also offers a chance to transform
			how we treat and prevent cancer.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<h2>About the Network</h2>
		<p>
			The International CIN Network (iCIN) originated while writing a community consensus on
			challenges we face in CIN research. In doing so, we identified seven challenges across three
			domains of CIN research and a roadmap for addressing them. The mission of iCIN is to foster
			collaboration within the CIN community to solve these challenges.
		</p>
	</section>
</SectionLayout>
